.SH "NAME"
PKGBUILD \- Lotus Package Definition File (LPDF v1)
.SS "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
A \fIPKGBUILD\fP is the file \fBlpm\fP(1) builds a package from. Lotus uses a single
definition format \(em \fILPDF v1\fP \(em with no legacy syntax, no fallback format,
and no migration path: a PKGBUILD that does not follow this spec simply fails
to parse.
.sp
The file is a bash script. \fIlpm\fP reads its fields with a fast native C parser
first; \fIbash\fP is only actually executed for the \fIbuild()\fP, \fIcheck()\fP, and
\fIpackage()\fP functions (and as a fallback parser if the fast parser cannot read
the file).
.sp
Canonical key style is \fIkey = "value"\fP (spaces around \fI=\fP). \fIkey="value"\fP is
also accepted but is not the documented form.
.sp
The PKGBUILD for a package named \fIfoo\fP is stored on disk as \fIpkgbuild_foo\fP.
.SS "FILE RULES"
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Comments run from \fI#\fP to end of line, as in bash.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fI$\f(CR{\fPpkgver}\fP and \fI$\f(CR{\fPpkgname}\fP may be used inside string values and
array elements, expanded against values already defined \fIearlier\fP in the same
file. Forward references do not work.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Field order does not matter to the parser, except that a variable must be
assigned before another value expands it with \fI$\f(CR{\fP.\|.\|.}\fP.
.RE
.SS "SCALAR FIELDS"
.sp
\fIpkgname\fP (required)
.RS 4
Package name. Lowercase ASCII \fIa\-z\fP, digits, \fI\-\fP and \fI_\fP only; must start
with a letter or digit. \fIlpm\fP matches dependency names with a
case\-sensitive \fIstrcmp()\fP, so "Foo" and "foo" are unrelated and a
mis\-cased name silently never resolves.
.RE
.sp
\fIpkgver\fP (required)
.RS 4
Upstream version, and nothing else. Digits, letters, \fI.\fP and \fI\-\fP only. \fINo\fP
\fIv\fP/\fIV\fP prefix (strip \fIv1.2.2\fP to \fI1.2.2\fP): \fIversion_compare()\fP splits on
\fI.\fP and \fI\-\fP and a stray \fIv\fP breaks the comparison. Epochs are supported as
\fIepoch:version\fP (e.g. \fI2:1.2.2\fP) and compared numerically first; use only
when upstream\(cqs scheme breaks ordering.
.RE
.sp
\fIpkgrel\fP (required)
.RS 4
Packaging revision \(em a positive integer as a string ("1", "2", .\|.\|.); no
decimals, letters, or leading zeroes. Bump this (not \fIpkgver\fP) when you
change the PKGBUILD itself; reset to "1" when \fIpkgver\fP changes.
.RE
.sp
\fIpkgdesc\fP (recommended)
.RS 4
One\-line description. No trailing period.
.RE
.sp
\fIlicense\fP (recommended)
.RS 4
SPDX identifier or short name (\fIMIT\fP, \fIGPL\-3.0\-only\fP, \fIApache\-2.0\fP). Single
value; for multi\-licensed software pick the binding one.
.RE
.sp
\fIpkgtype\fP (optional)
.RS 4
"source" (default) or "binary". Anything else is treated as "source".
\fIbinary\fP tells \fIlpm\fP to fetch a prebuilt \fI.lpkg\fP instead of running
\fIbuild()\fP/\fIpackage()\fP.
.RE
.SS "ARRAY FIELDS"
.sp
Bash arrays; \fIomit any you don\(cqt need\fP \(em absent is the same as empty.
Elements are separated by whitespace (a comma or semicolon between them is
tolerated but never required). Limits: 128 for the dependency/metadata arrays,
64 for \fIbackup\fP.
.sp
\fIdepends\fP
.RS 4
Runtime dependencies. Element is "name" or "name>=ver", "name\(lAver",
"name=ver". Only \fI>=\fP, \fI\(lA\fP, and \fI=\fP are supported; strict \fI>\fP and \fI<\fP are
not (use \fI>=\fP/\fI\(lA\fP, or omit the operator for "any version").
.RE
.sp
\fImakedepends\fP
.RS 4
Build\-time\-only dependencies. Not required at runtime.
.RE
.sp
\fIrecommends\fP
.RS 4
Soft dependencies: offered at \fIinstall\fP time, never force\-installed, never
blocking if absent.
.RE
.sp
\fIconflicts\fP
.RS 4
Packages that cannot be installed alongside this one.
.RE
.sp
\fIprovides\fP
.RS 4
Virtual capability names this package satisfies.
.RE
.sp
\fIreplaces\fP
.RS 4
Old package name(s) this package supersedes (rename support).
.RE
.sp
\fIgroups\fP
.RS 4
Arbitrary bulk\-operation tags (e.g. "toolchain"). Not used for dependency
resolution.
.RE
.sp
\fIbackup\fP
.RS 4
Config file paths, \fIrelative to the install root\fP, that survive
upgrades/reinstalls instead of being overwritten.
.RE
.SS "SOURCES AND CHECKSUMS"
.sp
\fIsources\fP and \fIchecksums\fP are \fIpositionally paired\fP arrays: \fIchecksums[i]\fP
describes \fIsources[i]\fP, same index, no exceptions. Insert a new checksum at the
same position you insert a new source; appending one at the end pairs it with
the wrong source.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
sources = (
    "https://example.org/foo\-${pkgver}.tar.xz"
    "patches/musl\-fix.patch"
)

checksums = (
    "sha512:aabbcc...."
    "SKIP"
)
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Each \fIsources\fP element is a full URL (fetched over the network) or a path
relative to the PKGBUILD\(cqs directory (used as\-is \(em typically a patch). The
parser never rewrites these strings.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Each \fIchecksums\fP element is one of: \fIsha512:<128 hex>\fP, \fIsha256:<64 hex>\fP,
\fImd5:<32 hex>\fP, or \fISKIP\fP (explicitly unverified \(em for your own patch files,
never for upstream tarballs). The algorithm is read \fIentirely from the
prefix\fP; there is no auto\-detection by length.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
A missing checksum (fewer entries than sources) is treated as unverified with
a warning \(em not a hard error. Do not rely on this.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Up to 32 entries in each of \fIsources\fP / \fIchecksums\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fIRemoved, never coming back:\fP \fIsource\fP, \fIsource2\fP, \fIsource3\fP, \fIsha256sums\fP,
\fIsha512sums\fP, \fImd5sums\fP. A PKGBUILD using any of those has \fIzero\fP
sources/checksums as far as \fIlpm\fP is concerned \(em a silent empty result, not a
warning.
.SS "BUILD FUNCTIONS"
.sp
\fIbuild()\fP (optional)
.RS 4
Compile. Runs unprivileged in \fI$srcdir\fP. Omit for packages needing no
build step.
.RE
.sp
\fIcheck()\fP (optional)
.RS 4
Test suite. Runs unprivileged. Informational unless \fI\-\-strict\fP makes a
failure fatal, or unless run explicitly via \fIlpm test\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fIpackage()\fP (required)
.RS 4
Stage the final install tree into \fI$pkgdir\fP (e.g. \fImake DESTDIR="$pkgdir"
install\fP). This is the only function \fIlpm\fP requires \(em everything installed
comes from what ends up under \fI$pkgdir\fP.
.RE
.sp
Inside these functions the available variables are \fI$srcdir\fP (extracted
sources), \fI$pkgdir\fP (staging root), and \fI$\f(CR{\fPpkgname}\fP / \fI$\f(CR{\fPpkgver}\fP /
\fI$\f(CR{\fPpkgrel}\fP.
.sp
Each of \fIbuild()\fP, \fIcheck()\fP, and \fIpackage()\fP runs with the working directory
reset to the build workspace root \fIindependently\fP: never assume shell state
(such as a \fIcd\fP) carries from one function to the next. For out\-of\-tree builds,
create the build directory inside \fIbuild()\fP and \fIcd\fP into the same relative
path again at the top of \fIcheck()\fP / \fIpackage()\fP.
.sp
\fIuninstall()\fP is \fInot\fP supported. \fIlpm\fP detects it for diagnostics but never
calls it; removal is handled entirely through the recorded file list.
.SS "EXAMPLE"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
# Lotus Package Definition File (LPDF v1)
# Filename: PKGBUILD
# Encoding: UTF\-8

pkgname = "hello"
pkgver\&  = "2.12.1"
pkgrel\&  = "1"
pkgdesc = "The GNU Hello package"
license = "GPL\-3.0"

pkgtype\&     = "source"
depends\&     = ("musl" "busybox")
makedepends = ("gcc" "make")
recommends\&  = ("man\-pages")
conflicts\&   = ("hello\-bin")
provides\&    = ("virtual\-hello")
replaces\&    = ("hello\-old")
groups\&      = ("gnu\-utils")
backup\&      = ("etc/hello.conf")

sources = (
    "https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/hello/hello\-2.12.1.tar.gz"
    "hello\-fix\-musl.patch"
)

checksums = (
    "sha256:8d99142afd92576f30b0cd7cb42a8dc6809998bc5d607d88761f512e26c7db20"
    "SKIP"
)

build() {
    cd "hello\-${pkgver}"
    patch \-p1 < ../hello\-fix\-musl.patch
    ./configure \-\-prefix=/usr
    make
}

check() {
    cd "hello\-${pkgver}"
    make check
}

package() {
    cd "hello\-${pkgver}"
    make DESTDIR="$pkgdir" install
}
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "SEE ALSO"
